Track 3: The Ghost-Lighter of Port Etienne
- Story & Context: Local myth claims that during the great neap tide of 1979, an abandoned iron barge adrift in the ship graveyard outside Port Etienne mysteriously broadcasted a looping, frantic guitar transmission across maritime VHF Channel 16. Dockworkers who rowed out through the freezing sea-fog found only a pair of split rawhide congas, a puddle of brine, and a battery-operated transistor synth baking under the Saharan coastal sun, its keys held down by dried sea sponges. Every winter since, when the desert wind blows sand out across the harbor swell, fishermen on the night watch still sing Kofi's haunting closing refrain, "The desert traded you a keyboard, but the Atlantic kept your lungs," believing it keeps the ghost vessels from crushing their skiffs.
